<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emotan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1440">1440</a>–<a href="/years/1473">1473</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Uwaraye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emotan, born Uwaraye in Eyaen, was a market woman who sold foodstuffs around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> Market in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> kingdom. After her husband's death she built a hut where she cared for children, the first day-care centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>. Oral history holds that she warned the exiled Prince Ogun of a murder plot by Oba Uwaifiokun and some chiefs, helping him take the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare the Great</a>, who made her Iyeki, charged with enforcing the market rules. After her death Ewuare deified her and had a sacred Uruhe tree planted where she had traded; a bronze statue of her was unveiled at the Oba Market in 1954.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Women also traded: tradition remembers <b>Emotan</b>, a market trader who helped Ewuare win back the throne.</blockquote><a href="/societies/benin">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</a></li></ul></section>
